"""Preserve independent English arguments; restore their concrete narrative evidence."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];D=R/'data/and-quiet-flows-the-don';M=D/'multilingual'
original=json.loads((M/'source-original.json').read_text())['articles']
adds={
1:{1:["The background is a particular bargain, not merely a fondness for home. Don Cossack households possess land and a privileged military status, while their men owe service and must equip themselves with horses and other necessities. A courtyard that feeds a family also prepares soldiers. When the First World War arrives, the state's claim reaches into that ordinary life. Grigori is Pantelei Melekhov's younger son, raised inside this arrangement before he begins to doubt the causes that recruit him.","His first killing of an Austrian soldier makes the violence bodily. The man is not only an opposing uniform: Grigori has approached, struck, and seen what he has done. The victim's image troubles him. At a later stage the novel also describes his heart hardening and his early pity disappearing. His heart can harden, yet remorse returns, making it difficult to keep military action separate from the human being it destroys."],2:["Another confrontation concerns prisoners. While fighting with the Reds, Grigori witnesses the killing of captured White officers under Podtyolkov. They are already disarmed. He tries to intervene and cannot stop the slaughter. A claim to justice has become a reason to kill men who can no longer fight back. It is a lasting source of estrangement, not a theoretical proposition he has first worked out and then applied.","The Cossack side offers no clean refuge from the treatment of captives as disposable. Daria, Petro's widow, shoots the captive Ivan Alekseyevich, and another Cossack follows with a bayonet. Grigori’s brother Petro is himself killed after surrendering to Red forces; the killer, Mikhail Koshevoi, comes from their village. Grigori's grief also becomes violence. His own furious attacks and subsequent collapse prevent the reader from treating him as a detached observer of other people's crimes. The same war makes him victim, commander, avenger, and perpetrator."],5:["Fomin's band brings the contradiction to its bleakest point. After returning home and later fleeing a warning of impending arrest, Grigori joins a force whose raids and violence he cannot honestly mistake for a satisfactory political home. Knowing that a road is wrong does not supply another road on which to live. Nor does the absence of safety make the actions taken there cease to be his."]},
2:{1:["Marriage to Stepan did not replace that early violence with security: he beat her, treating an injury done to her as a stain for which she could be punished. When she and Grigori become lovers, his father opposes them and arranges his marriage to Natalia. Aksinia and Grigori eventually leave the village to work on the Listnitsky estate, she in domestic service and he with the horses. The scandalous relation becomes, for a time, a shared working life."],2:["Natalia's marriage has the approval that theirs lacks. That does not protect her from Grigori's emotional absence. She attempts to cut her own throat and survives with an injury. Later the couple have twins, Mishatka and Polyushka. When she again learns of his relationship with Aksinia, she seeks an abortion and dies from blood loss. The contrast is not between a false wife and a true lover. It is between public recognition, the actual conduct of a relationship, and the injuries for which that conduct remains responsible."],3:["Their bond is not uninterrupted. While Grigori is away at war, Aksinia loses their young daughter and becomes involved with the estate owner's son. Grigori responds with violence and returns to Natalia. Later he and Aksinia renew their attachment. The repeated departures and returns matter to the essay's reading: there is no approved arrangement that can simply keep this relation in place regardless of what its participants do. Late in the novel Dunyashka urges Grigori to marry Aksinia and says their mother had wanted it too. Private approval arrives, but it cannot undo their losses or make them politically safe.","After Natalia's death Aksinia also cares for Grigori's children. Care does not cancel the earlier injury; it puts another act beside it. Finally, when Grigori asks her to leave with him, she agrees to an uncertain flight rather than remain in the more settled life available in the village. They are neither protected by their love nor reduced to the community's judgment of it."],4:["A grain-requisitioning patrol challenges the pair as they ride at night. They turn away and flee; the sentries fire. Aksinia is mortally wounded and dies in Grigori's arms before dawn. He digs her grave with his sabre in the morning light. When he looks up, the sun appears black. This is not an untargeted bullet wandering into a private idyll: armed pursuit has reached the two people trying to escape it."]},
3:{1:["The first volume gives that network material weight: Pantelei's courtyard, Ilyinichna's meals, two sons and a daughter, livestock, land, work, quarrels, and marriages. The later losses are devastating because the novel has already shown what each person did there. A list can count the dead; only those early scenes can show why the emptied places are not interchangeable."],2:["Petro surrenders and is shot by Koshevoi. He appeals for help to Ivan Alekseyevich, his children's godfather, who is present; the appeal does not save him. Natalia dies after an abortion, Daria drowns herself after contracting syphilis, and Pantelei dies of typhus during retreat. Ilyinichna declines while longing for Grigori's return. Polyushka dies of diphtheria. Aksinia is killed by gunfire during flight. Executions and flight belong beside illness and grief in the household's losses.","The distinction matters more than a statistic. War destroys through executions and pursuit, but also through displacement, disease, exhaustion, and the disintegration of arrangements that sustain a household. Natalia's death cannot be detached from her husband's betrayal and assigned to an impersonal war alone. The larger catastrophe works through particular decisions and vulnerabilities, not instead of them."],3:["That new order is inseparable from a familiar face. Koshevoi is not a stranger sent into the village for one scene of violence. He has known these people, becomes a local Soviet official, and marries the sister of the man he killed. Ilyinichna initially resists his entry into the family. Dunyashka nevertheless insists on the attachment. Political judgment, affection, resentment, and the need to keep living occupy the same courtyard."],4:["Nor has Dunyashka severed her bond with her surviving brother: she offers to vacate the old house for him, and they affirm their affection. Political hostility inside a household is not the same as the disappearance of every family tie. Her care for Grigori's children also continues across these fractures. Polyushka does not survive, but Mishatka does; Dunyashka herself is alive at the end. The final father-and-son image is therefore not a literal census of the household's only two survivors. It concentrates Grigori's remaining connection to life into one embrace, while the novel has left other living relations, however damaged, around it."]},
4:{1:["The land question gives these claims their force. Cossack families understand land and status through generations of military service; poorer outsiders experience the same order as exclusion. Revolutionary coercion and executions then help turn the upper Don against Soviet power. The uprising cannot be understood either as mere irrational attachment to privilege or as an innocent community with no stake in an unequal arrangement."],2:["The captured officers killed under Podtyolkov give this refusal a concrete scene. Grigori cannot accept that surrender has made no difference to what may be done to a man. Yet this is a judgment to which he returns, not a boundary he never crosses. His own killings, his rage after Petro's death, and his participation in armed groups remain in the account. Remorse is evidence of conflict, not a certificate of immunity from responsibility."],3:["A return to his own village does not settle the matter. Koshevoi, now joined to the household through Dunyashka, remains suspicious of his past. Dunyashka remains attached to her brother, but a warning that he will be arrested sends him away again; he eventually finds himself with Fomin. The band offers movement and armed company rather than an answer he can believe in. He sees that its course is destructive and still participates. The passage is especially harsh because insight and conduct have come apart."],5:["The essay's distinction is between having no concern and having a concern that no available allegiance can securely carry. Calling both conditions instability misses something important. But claiming that Grigori has always lived up to his concern would miss something equally important. The novel leaves the refusal and the failure together. He is not simply choosing between two lectures; neither is he exempt from judgment because both lecturers have blood on their hands."]},
5:{1:["Aksinia has died during their attempted flight. Grigori spends further time hiding, including a period with deserters, but cannot bear remaining apart from his children. Instead of waiting for the amnesty they discuss, he turns homeward. At the Don he throws away his rifle and Nagant revolver and empties out the cartridges. He crosses on the March ice. Disarmament does not erase his history; it makes this return different from another movement under arms."],2:["On the slope near the landing he sees Mishatka. The boy recognizes him but lowers his eyes in fear. Grigori approaches and takes him in his arms. He learns that Polyushka died of diphtheria the previous autumn; Dunyashka is well, and Koshevoi has gone into military service. The scene does not say that every other relative has vanished. It shows how little can now answer the longing that brought him back."],3:["The closing image is the father holding his son at the gate of his own home. The narrator presents this as what remains to connect him to the immense, shining world beneath a cold sun. Brightness has not disappeared with his losses. The landscape's continuance neither compensates him nor waits for a verdict on his past. That conjunction is why the embrace cannot comfortably become an emblem of historical reconciliation."],4:["Land, rank, military honors, and political standing have depended on institutions capable of granting or withdrawing them. The child's claim upon his father is not awarded by the victorious army. This does not mean children cannot die, or that parenthood is free of law and social recognition. Polyushka's death forbids the first simplification. What remains is the immediate obligation of this person to this child, not a guarantee that either will be protected."]}}
replacements={(3,1):('Family extinction becomes a process rather than an event.','The unraveling of a household becomes a process rather than an event.'),(2,4):('Grigori buries her before dawn','Grigori buries her after dawn'),(5,3):('parents, siblings, or home','his parents, his brother, or the old household')}
titles=['He Fought on Both Sides','The Illicit One','How a Family Was Dismantled','Why He Refused to Choose','He Lifted His Son']
decks=['Concrete loyalties amid changing uniforms.','An illicit bond, a legitimate marriage, and the people neither judgment may erase.','A household loses its members through different routes.','A recurring moral concern is not an unbroken record of innocence.','A return to a child without a promise of safety.']
